---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import GifAudioVoiceRecorder from '@/components/tools/media/GifAudioVoiceRecorder.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="voice-recorder">
  <GifAudioVoiceRecorder client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to record your voice</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Press record and allow microphone access when the browser asks. The permission is for this site only, and the voice recorder captures nothing before you press the button.</li>
      <li>Watch the waveform and the level bar. Speech should move the bar to between 40% and 80%. A bar that barely moves means the gain is too low; a bar at 100% means clipping.</li>
      <li>Pause and resume as often as you like; the segments join without gaps.</li>
      <li>Stop, listen in the player, then download the file as it is or convert it to MP3 or WAV.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Where the recording is kept</h2>
    <p>
      The browser’s <code>MediaRecorder</code> gives a chunk of encoded audio once a second, and this tab keeps
      the chunks in an array in memory. When you press stop, they are joined into one Blob for the player.
      Nothing is written to disk until you press download, and nothing is sent: the site’s
      Content-Security-Policy lists no third-party origin.
    </p>
    <p>
      So if you reload or close the tab, the recording is lost. No draft is saved anywhere else.
    </p>

    <h2>Recording format, and when to convert</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Browser</th><th>Container and codec</th><th>Roughly</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Opera</td><td>WebM containing Opus</td><td>30 MB per hour</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Safari (macOS and iOS)</td><td>MP4 containing AAC</td><td>45 MB per hour</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Both are complete, playable files. Convert only when the destination needs it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>MP3 for older software, a car stereo, a voicemail system, or a transcription service with a limited decoder.</li>
      <li>WAV for a DAW or sampler, where uncompressed PCM avoids repeated decoding. See <a href="/mp3-to-wav">MP3 to WAV</a> for the size cost.</li>
      <li>Neither if you keep the file or send it to someone with a modern device. WebM and MP4 audio play in every current browser and phone.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The three processing switches</h2>
    <p>
      By default, browsers apply voice-call signal processing to microphone streams. The advanced panel has a
      switch for each of the three, because the right setting depends on what you record.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Leave on for</th><th>Turn off for</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Echo cancellation</td>
            <td>Recordings through laptop speakers, or with audio playing</td>
            <td>Music and instruments: it can reduce sustained notes as if they were echo</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Noise suppression</td>
            <td>Speech in a room with fans, traffic or air conditioning</td>
            <td>Singing, acoustic instruments, ambience you want to keep, very quiet speech</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Automatic gain control</td>
            <td>Interviews where people move around, or when levels vary</td>
            <td>Performances with intentional dynamics; it flattens loud and quiet parts</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Getting a better recording</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Distance matters more than equipment. A cheap headset mic 10 cm from your mouth sounds better than an expensive one across the room, because room reflections make recordings sound amateur.</li>
      <li>Speak past the microphone, not into it. A position slightly off-axis avoids the plosive blast on p and b sounds.</li>
      <li>Avoid reflective surfaces. A recording made facing a bookshelf or a curtain sounds much better than one facing a bare wall or a window.</li>
      <li>Test first. The <a href="/microphone-test">microphone test</a> checks levels and playback in a few seconds, before a long take.</li>
      <li>Record a few seconds of silence at the start. Noise-reduction tools in editors use it as a profile.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Is there a time limit?</h2>
    <p>
      The tool sets no time limit. The real limit is memory: at about 30 MB an hour for Opus, a session of
      several hours works on a desktop and less well on an older phone. For sessions over about two hours,
      stop and start a new take from time to time, in case the browser crashes. You can join the takes later
      with the <a href="/audio-joiner">audio joiner</a>, losslessly if they are all the same format.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No system audio: <code>getUserMedia</code> gives only microphone inputs. To capture what your computer plays, you need screen-capture permission with audio sharing, and on macOS a loopback driver.</li>
      <li>One microphone at a time: a multitrack session needs a DAW and an audio interface.</li>
      <li>No transcription: that needs a speech model, which means a large download or a server.</li>
      <li>No recovery after a reload: the tool stores nothing, by design.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
